[03/06/25]
Had a very good morning today and even started to try and solve the problems with sandwiches. Here's the game plan:
I want to re-write the parser to not continually go through the file character by character; instead, iterate through the file until the beginning of either a variable name, variable value, class type, class name, etc. Then, process that entire line each line at a time. This will make things a lot less succeptible to small issues causing major problems. It will also make accounting for things like sandwiches easier. The idea for sandwiches is to process the sandwich line all at once, expanding it into multiple keys and values, with the value types being the type + the sandwich type. That way, the interpreter can detect when a variable is a sandwich while also being able to find its original type. The most important thing to note is that the re-writing of the parser shouldn't require a re-write of the interpreter, it should give it the same data but in a much better fashion. The only major changes might be to sandwiches, but that's expected.

Okay, here's what I've got right now:
The parser checks a boolean to determine if it's inside curly brackets or not. If it is, then the loop is roughly as follows:

If at a closed curly bracket, toggle the boolean, add object_settings to all_settings, and clear all variables that need to be cleared/reset.

If at a beginning value delimiter ('<' or '[' or '('), parse the data, set the value of object_settings[current_setting_index] to that value, and set the value type to += the type of the current value.

If at the sandwich delimiter, break.

If at any non-whitespace character that's not inside a value delimiter, parse it as a key and insert a new key/value pair into object_settings. However, if encountering the sandwich delimiter, set the value type of the newly inserted pair to be SANDWICH - THEATRE_REFERENCE.

