Okay, I'm actually going to just jump into the VBOs stuff first; get that over with. My thought process is that since mesh data is always something that's roughly static, meaning that mesh data usually doesn't change either at all or very often. What this means for me is that I should load and store all the mesh data that the engine can currently access, and then once rendering is to commence, I check that the meshes I need are all buffered. Oh my god, I also just realized that I could've made my life a lot easier... I can just have mesh data be buffered on demand instead of all at once. This would make sure that I only buffer the mesh data that needs to be buffered. I don't know if clearing all the VBOs every time a new Theatre is loaded is a good idea but regardless of when and how I choose to flush unused buffers, checking/creating them on demand is a much better idea (at least, I think). Before I start coding, however, I do need to answer that question of how/when I'm going to flush the VBOs. It would be more efficient (and lead to better load times) if I don't just flush them all and re-buffer the ones I need, but that method is also the simplest so it'll be a last resort fallback option. I could just use the method I came up with where I add all the current Mesh objects' mesh data identifiers into one big string and then do an std::string::find on every stored mesh data identifier, but that's a bit pedantic. Oh! Wait a minute, I already semi-fixed this problem. Flushing every VBO and rebuilding them on every Theatre load might be inefficient, but setting a boolean to false and then setting it to true is much less inefficient. All that's left is actually flushing buffers. I need to know when it's time to check the booleans (i.e: they've all been set to their final values); if I'm going to be buffering the required VBOs on demand, then there's not going to be a function for checking and buffering them. However, that means that I now don't know when they've all been checked. Maybe instead of a boolean, it could be an int and I could check for three values with two of them being effectively true or false, but the third value being "not yet checked". Yeah, I think I'll do that! I also want to change how mesh data is stored and how VBOs are stored/accessed.

