Okay, I did a lot of work; I'm finally at the point where I can start coding the VBO stuff, LMFAO. Remind me to never get high before work, even a little bit. Anyways, I did get a lot of actually good stuff done. Mainly, better compartmentalization/abstraction that is healthy for the codebase (not abstracting just to abstract). I created my own implementation of a "Vector3" type that only takes unsigned integers; this is to help make the processing of indices resemble the processing of vertices in MeshData. MeshData is an infinitely better implementation of gMeshData; gMeshData used to be a simple typedef, then a small struct, then a big struct and as such it kept a lot of the same ideas and styles of simpler containers. This led to it not fitting its actual purpose very well. MeshData, on the other hand, was designed from the ground up with the failings of gMeshData in mind; some of the improvements made include: way more helper functions, better and more detailed functions for getting the size of the data (both the number of combined elements and the total bytes of the data), way better constructors, specific helper functions for adding whole vertices (position+normal+uv+color) at once, and more. MeshData also has VBO and IBO member variables for holding buffer names, as well as a VAO_index variable for storing the (you guessed it) VAO index in VAOs that it uses (e.g: VAOs[VAO_index]).

In conclusion, I've finished building the ground work for what comes next: the more ardurous process of re-writing the rendering pipeline.

