[03/30/25]
Found where the segmentation fault is happening; it's in `graphx::gClass(std::string init_name)` right here:
:::C++
if(valid_classes.contains(init_name))
:::
I'm pretty sure that any time `valid_classes` is iterated on or otherwise interacted with (i.e: `.contains` in this case), one of the operations in std::set (I'm assuming a constructor or destructor) starts an infinite loop of graphx::gClass constructors.

I think it's to do with the conversion operators; `.contains()` expects a reference to an object of the same type as the set contains, but since I pass it either a string or an int, it needs to create a new object using that string or int. Since that constructor is the same one, however, it results in a feedback loop and a stack overflow.

Oh god... is it because I forgot to make sure that the constructors add items to the set??? Is it because the set is empty???? No, that can't be the real reason...

<interjecting on 04/02/25>
I forgot to write more notes, but it turns out that I just really didn't know what I wanted from graphx::gClass and was just going down rabbit hole after rabbit hole. In the end, `valid_classes` was renamed to graphx::gClasses::classes bc... no reason, honestly, but more importantly, it's defined in `graphx_classes_namespace.hpp` after all the gClass objects in graphx::classes are. This means that while it's not automatically filled in by each object when they're created (which I couldn't realistically do, anyways, due to how and why I created the gClass objects in the first place), it's only one extra line of code (really just copying a variable name and pasting it into a vector), and it's right below where you'd define the valid gClass objects. These are also now the only two places that need to be updated in order for the engine to recognize a new Actor/Device, which is a far far FAR cry (haha) better than before, where you'd need to write up to four completely unique lines of code in three/four completely different files. Total nightmare turned super easy task, and all with a custom type that can be compared against an integer/float or a string; not too bad if I do say so myself.

