So far I've made adding new Actors/Devices to the engine much easier by taking the process from this:
  1: Create integer type identifier inside graphx::classes, in graphx_namespace.hpp
  2: Add that identifier with a string key to the graphx::classnames map, in graphx_namespace.hpp
  3: Add that identifier with a function pointer to either actor_map in g_actors.cpp or device_map in r_common.cpp

To this:
  1: Create graphx::gClass type identifier inside graphx::classes, in graphx_classes_namespace.hpp
  2: Add that identifier to graphx::gClass::classes, defined in graphx_classes_namespace.hpp

The process has gone from three completely different lines of code in two completely separate files to two simple lines of code (one of which is just copying a variable name into a vector) which are both in the same file (as well as conveniently right next to eachother).

I've also used Heaptrack to find and fix a few of the worst memory leaks (I'm talking MB of memory...); while there are still quite a few leaks to plug up, the largest ones only get to ~200kb and are from instantiation/setup functions (meaning, memory isn't leaking every frame), so it's no longer a huge priority, although it will be a continued, consistent thing I'll try and maintain (like spring cleaning).

All this to say, I think I can merge this branch and move on to implementing some minor features that I could easily get done tonight (i.e: simple debug menu interaction?).

Added proof of concept for interactive debugging menu! I can now change the global position of every Actor in a Theatre. Certain Actors like PhysicsActors will immediately override changes to their position, which is why the actual implementation of an interactive debugger will use a custom interface that can pick and choose which variables it presents based on the class. A raycast picker function would also be nice, as right now it goes through a semi-accurate list of every Actor (the list is updated supposedly every time a new Theatre is loaded). This implementation is garbage and bad but it's a proof of concept for a much better, fuller implementation.

